{
  "image_name": "3bdafcc1819b245a489b312c234ff97c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates a proposed signaling pathway linking liver injury to activation of hepatic stellate cells and fibrotic collagen production. After injury, hepatocytes appear to release increased amounts of osteopontin and HMGB1, shown as red and green dots moving into the extracellular space. These molecules then act on the stellate cell above.\n\nWithin the stellate cell, osteopontin seems to signal through RAGE and PI3K to activate AKT1/2/3, which is marked by phosphorylation. Activated AKT promotes HMGB1 movement and acetylation, leading to its extracellular release. HMGB1 is also shown inside the nucleus and cytoplasm, suggesting a shift from intracellular to secreted forms. Another branch shows osteopontin increasing NOX, which inhibits HDAC1/2; this likely favors HMGB1 acetylation and release as well.\n\nThe overall consequence is stimulation of type I collagen production, indicated by the collagen fibers exiting the stellate cell. In essence, the figure presents a feed-forward profibrotic mechanism in which liver injury causes hepatocytes to release osteopontin and HMGB1, these signals activate stellate cells through RAGE/PI3K/AKT and NOX/HDAC-related pathways, and the activated stellate cells generate collagen associated with fibrosis.",
